  "evidence": "TEXT_D: “strong internationalism”; support the EU Constitution and prepare to join the euro; repeal control orders and oppose ID cards; prosecute terror in mainstream courts; Single Equality Act and hate-crime units; allow asylum seekers to work and speed asylum on fairness grounds; green economy with carbon tax, 20% renewables, no new nuclear, congestion charging, zero-waste, oppose new SE airports; electoral reform (STV), votes at 16, predominantly elected upper chamber; multilateral disarmament and climate leadership.\nTEXT_G: Leave the EU, repeal the European Communities Act, reassert UK legal supremacy; immigration aiming for near‑zero net migration with Britishness tests, tighter family reunion, safe‑third‑country rule, faster removals, and amending human rights law; repeal the Human Rights Act; uphold Parliament, monarchy, common law, and the (independent) House of Lords; repeal the Hunting Act; tougher sentencing and more prison capacity; deploy forces only in the national interest; build new nuclear, stop wind rollout; promote responsibility, family, and opposition to “political correctness.”",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_D argues from social liberalism, civil liberties, environmentalism, and internationalism—core GAL commitments—while TEXT_G argues from national sovereignty, traditional institutions, restrictive immigration, and law‑and‑order—core TAN commitments. Because the electorate profile leans TAN (0.60) over GAL (0.40), the strongly TAN‑oriented TEXT_G more closely matches the profile than the strongly GAL‑oriented TEXT_D.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
